Oxide Rubato

Ingredients: FREE JAZZ / AVANT-GARDE + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + Harmonica  
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a waterlogged, corrupted cassette discovered inside an unmapped 1974 municipal time capsule.

Oxide Rubato sounds like an avant-garde improvisational ensemble playing at a sleepy 2:00 AM lullaby volume inside a decaying tape player. Atonal cluster chords and freeform, non-metric harmonica multiphonics drift lazily over wool-wrapped upright bass plucks, warped Rhodes electric piano voicings, and the tactile hiss of flaking iron oxide. It transforms the chaotic, confrontational energy of free jazz into an intimate, dust-dappled living room shelter—comforting yet harmonically bewildering.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 46–64 BPM. The rhythm rejects the grid entirely; drums perform a slack, unquantized rubato pulse with brush-scraped snares, uncoupled kick thuds, and yawning pauses that breathe rather than loop.
- Harmonic Palette: Dissonant microtonal warmth. Major-seventh chords smeared with minor seconds, flatted ninths, bitonal modal washes, and microtonally bent harmonica overtones that hover uncomfortably yet gently between major and minor thirds.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Thermal Azimuth Smearing: Tape playback heads are kept at human body temperature (37°C) to induce real-time physical magnetic binder drag, turning sharp transient attacks into slow, velvet smears.
  2. Boiled-Wool Harmonica Muffling: Diatonic and chromatic harmonicas are wrapped tightly in dampened boiled wool felt, choking their bright brassiness to make them sound like wheezing wooden chamber organs.
  3. Unrepaired Dropouts: Exact micro-second gaps and flutter hiccups from the original time-capsule tape are built into the drum breaks as rhythmic syncopations.
- Dynamics: Wavering and domestic. Tracks breathe through long, whispered hums and gentle flutter swells, occasionally surging into warm clouds of multiphonic reed saturation before evaporating back to bedroom room-tone.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the autumn of 2024, drainage workers unearthing a dried culvert beneath the border town of Oolteghem pried open a rusted municipal time capsule buried fifty years prior. Inside lay an unlabelled, mold-matted cassette tape whose ferric binder had melted into the polyester backing; when salvaged by local bedroom producers, the ruined tape spat out mangled, out-of-time harmonica solos floating over drowsy avant-garde jazz cadences. Within months, an insular youth movement formed in the river basin's damp, abandoned greenhouses. Adopting "Oxide Fashion"—heavy thrifted chore jackets edged with braided brown cassette tape, antique surveyor pins worn as lapel jewelry, and pocket-sized unamplified diatonic harmonicas—they hold nocturnal listening vigils, brewing bitter roasted chicory tea while practicing circular-breathing microtones over drifting, un-synced cassette decks.

Central Instruments: Boiled-wool dampened chromatic harmonica, water-damaged Rhodes Mark I, bowed gut-string upright double bass, brush kit with cracked splash cymbal, and antique dual-capstan reel-to-reel tape decks.  
